  "raw_html": "<button type='button' role='link' class='btn btn-link p-0 text-start' id='link_2' aria-label=\" Karnataka Power Transmission Corporation Limited versus Rekha & Ors pdf\"  class='noToken' href='#' onclick=javascript:open_pdf('2','2026','2026_8_489_505','2026INSC847'); ><font size='4'> <strong>KARNATAKA POWER TRANSMISSION CORPORATION LIMITED<span class='fst-italic'> versus </span>REKHA & ORS </strong>- <span class='escrText'>[2026] 8 S.C.R. 489</span><span class='ncDisplay'>2026 INSC 847</span><input type='hidden' id='cnr' value=ESCR010002742026></button></font><br><strong>Coram : SANJAY KAROL<sup style=\"color: #268e97;font-size: 22px;top: 0;\" class=\"tooltip-sup\" data-tooltip=\"Author\">*</sup>, N KOTISWAR SINGH</strong><br> Issue for Consideration Maintainability of writ petition in cases of electrocution; the yardstick to be used in determining compensation in such cases. Headnotes&dagger; Electrocution &ndash; Writ petition u/Art.226 &ndash; Maintainability &ndash; High Court directed the appellant-Corporation holding the writ petitions to be maintainable and the disputed facts to not be of much relevance because, according to it, it is the standard of absolute liability that is applicable and not strict liability &ndash; Interference with: Held: If questions of fact are disputed, then Art.226<br><strong class='caseDetailsTD' ><span style='color:#212F3D' > Decision Date :</span><font color='green'> 12-08-2026</font><span style='color:#212F3D' > | Case No :</span><font color='green'> CIVIL APPEAL No. 10659/2026</font><span style='color:#212F3D' > | Disposal Nature :</span><font color='green'> Appeal(s) allowed</font>   <span style='color:#212F3D' > |  Bench :</span><font color='green'> 2 Judges</font></strong><div class='row my-1 mt-2 border bg-light'><div class='col-auto'><a href='#' class='btn btn-outline-secondary btn-sm my-1' onclick=open_splitview('2','2026','2026_8_489_505','2026INSC847','S')><i <i class='fas fa-columns me-2'></i>Split view<